Here is an example of a Metal compute kernel that performs a Gaussian blur on an image:
```c++
#include <metal_stdlib>
using namespace metal;

kernel void gaussian_blur(
    texture2d<float, access::read> input [[texture(0)]],
    texture2d<float, access::write> output [[texture(1)]],
    constant float4& kernel [[buffer(0)]],
    uint2 gid [[thread_position_in_grid]]
) {
    // Define the size of the kernel
    const int kernel_size = 5;

    // Calculate the center of the kernel
    const int center_x = kernel_size / 2;
    const int center_y = kernel_size / 2;

    // Initialize the sum and count variables
    float sum = 0.0f;
    int count = 0;

    // Loop over the pixels in the neighborhood of the current pixel
    for (int y = -center_y; y <= center_y; ++y) {
        for (int x = -center_x; x <= center_x; ++x) {
            // Get the coordinates of the neighboring pixel
            int nx = gid.x + x;
            int ny = gid.y + y;

            // Check if the neighboring pixel is within bounds
            if (nx >= 0 && nx < input.get_width() &&
                ny >= 0 && ny < input.get_height()) {

                // Get the value of the neighboring pixel
                float val = input.read({nx, ny}).r;

                // Add the weighted value to the sum
                sum += val * kernel[count].w;

                // Increment the count variable
                ++count;
            }
        }
    }

    // Write the blurred pixel to the output texture
    output.write(float4(sum / count), gid);
}
```
This kernel takes as input a `texture2d` object representing the original image, a `texture2d` object representing the output image, and a `float4` array containing the weights for each pixel in the kernel. The kernel loops over the pixels in the neighborhood of the current pixel, calculates their weighted values using the kernel weights, and writes the resulting blurred pixel to the output texture.
Note that this is just one possible implementation of a Gaussian blur kernel in Metal. Depending on your specific use case, you may need to modify or optimize the code to achieve the desired results.

